WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.320
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.433
and I'm musician-in-residence at Yale

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.710
University's Fortunoff Video Archive for

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.560
Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.619
This video series is designed as a companion

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.780
to our album Where Is Our Homeland,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.299
which is the first volume in the archive's

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.819
ongoing effort to present songs

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.385
and poems performed by survivors in

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.529
testimonies given to the Fortunoff Archive

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.500
over the last 40 years.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.879
You can learn more about the project on the

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.740
archive's website,
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:07.879
Where Is Our Homeland?

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.139
Songs from Testimonies.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.319
Volume One was recorded in 2019

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.139
by the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble featuring

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.928
renowned singer Sasha Lurje, Craig

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.449
Judelman on the violin, Joshua Camp

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.939
on the accordion, keyboards and guitar,

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.029
Dmitry Ishenko on the bass, and myself

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.030
on woodwinds.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.955
The songs in this
collection differ vastly in

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.824
terms of style, socia and historical

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.739
background, language and many other

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.199
aspects, but collectively they

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.307
create a multidimensional picture of Jewish

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.299
life before and during World War

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.300
II.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.689
Jack M was one of the

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.067
wonderful folk singers who had a tremendous

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.969
memory, and he remembered a lot

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.069
of the pre-war repertoire

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.249
coming from the weddings, from

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.829
his home, from the army,

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.439
from the street, from the school.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.734
And his testimony not only

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.169
reveals his personal experience

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.305
during the Holocaust, but it really

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.479
gives us a really deep insight

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.629
into what Jewish Polish

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.410
culture was before the war.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.399
One of the songs that Jack sang

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.280
on the testimony is "Badkhen

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.199
Song." The badkhen was a Wedding

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.629
Jester, a figure that was really

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:31.216
instrumental in the Ashkenazic

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.419
Jewish wedding beginning in 1400s

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.639
and through the 20th century.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.620
As a boy, Jack remembered two badkhonen,

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.300
which is a plural of a Badkhen,

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.260
in his small town of Szydlowiec.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.860
One was a Hasidic religious person.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.969
The other was more of a secular

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:54.600
badkhen.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.169
And that song

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.263
was arranged by us, by the

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.569
Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble and performed

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.569
on the album Where Is Our Homeland

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.439
with the wonderful soloist singer Sasha

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.440
Lurje.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.919
So we decided to open it with

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.979
the Doina, which is a semi improvisatory

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.139
instrumental composition.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.529
This particular Doina came

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.020
from the collection of Moisey Beregovskiy,

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.629
a notable Ukrainian-Jewish Soviet

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.860
ethnographer, who captured along

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.410
with Sofia Magid, another

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.966
musicologist from
Leningrad, St. Petersburg,

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.499
the last moments of the

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.290
Old World pre-war Jewish tradition

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.630
in Belarus and Ukraine.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.229
So although Jack came from Poland,

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:49.660
but opening the composition with a Doina

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.380
makes it really a lifelike

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.600
music making situation.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:58.079
The song is followed by the freylekhs tune.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.616
The mocking street song
was "Vos bistu ketsele

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.100
baroygez." Why are you so

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:04.689
angry, honey?

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.580
The biggest kick off in a wedding

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.500
was the marshelik.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.140
A marshelik was out of this

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.180
world, he was the entertainer.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:23.853
He was the announcer of the

00:04:24.328 --> 00:04:26.629
[INAUDIBLE]. They didn't give no money,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.119
just presents for stay.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.100
In the wedding [? party ?].

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.260
When he got to entertain them in

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.780
dates, first we had two.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.999
One was a religious guy

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.660
and the other one was hartsveytik.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.819
Worldly, non-religious, or worldly.

00:04:44.559 --> 00:04:45.559
Yeah, yeah.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.700
The religious guy, he made it

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:49.710
in Hasidic.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.399
He gave them a Hasidic song.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.699
Eh, eh, keyn vitsin.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:56.809
No, no jokes at all.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:58.980
[INAUDIBLE]

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:02.660
Papa says to Shmelke:

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.220
"Don't be such a numbskull!

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.420
Heed to your old father.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.140
And restrain your anger with him."

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:17.860
He can't eat,

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.580
He can't sleep,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:21.740
For it's

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.100
written in the Torah,

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.300
Thus speaketh our creator:

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.780
"A Jew mustn't be alone.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.460
He must have a wife,

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:35.660
Who can later bury him.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:39.420
A Jew mustn't be alone."

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:11.060
Papa says to Shmelke:

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:15.580
"Don't be such a numbskull!

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:19.220
Heed to your old father.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:27.580
And restrain your anger with him."

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.820
He can't eat,

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.940
He can't sleep,

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:39.500
For it's

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.460
written in the Torah,

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:50.300
Thus speaketh our creator:

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:56.580
"A Jew mustn't be alone.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.300
He must have a wife,

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.620
Who can later bury him.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:10.060
A Jew mustn’t be alone."

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.300
For it's written in the Torah,

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:17.620
Thus speaketh our creator:

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:22.180
"A Jew mustn’t be alone.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.820
He must have a wife,

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.700
Who can later bury him.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:34.940
A Jew mustn't be alone."

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:52.780
The song of the marshelik,

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:55.700
the badkhen, which is

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.159
described as the biggest kick in a

00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.160
wedding.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:01.550
What's the context?

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:03.740
What's, what's going on here?

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:05.869
Well, it's, it's a wedding.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.380
And we know a

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:11.939
wedding has a groom and a bride and

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:14.849
the family and the

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.909
officiator, maybe a rabbi.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:21.380
Friends.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:24.940
Maybe some poor people are

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:27.300
being invited as well, depending

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:29.494
on how wealthy the family is.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.369
And there's one person who kind

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:36.789
of provides the glue, the continuity,

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:40.380
and that's the marshelik, the

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:41.620
badkhen.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:45.180
And the role of the badkhen

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:47.740
or the marshelik, as Jack

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:49.759
M. remembers him,

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.179
is to both entertain

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.100
and also instruct and keep

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:57.459
things moving.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:01.470
So there are

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:04.460
two of these marsheliks

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:07.190
that, that Jack remembers from his

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:09.900
growing up in pre-war

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:11.140
Eastern Europe.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:14.339
One of them was a religious guy

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.060
who was a Hasid and did things

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.300
sort of by the book straight.

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.300
And the other one he describes as

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.300
hartsveytik. That means it was already a

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:27.140
heartache.

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:28.780
And that

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:31.330
probably means that his parents

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:33.939
were caused a lot of heartache by his

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:36.340
straying from the path of religious

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:37.460
observance.

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.600
But on the other hand, it gave him that

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:42.465
point of view that made him a great

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.940
entertainer because he took things with

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:47.330
a grain of salt and he knew how to make

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.419
clever double entendres and

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:51.420
jokes.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.439
And it was very
entertaining to the point that

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:56.930
Jack remembers him as

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:59.209
providing the biggest kick in a wedding.

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:02.130
So.

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:05.550
In today's weddings, I think

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:07.170
we would

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:10.660
place this somewhere in

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:12.980
the area of a toast to

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.700
the couple, which sometimes turns

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.299
into a roast because

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.870
the family and friends who are celebrating

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.269
the marriage with the

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.549
man and wife to be, or wife and

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:29.740
wife, or man and man,

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:33.060
are often

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:35.839
making little inside jokes and referring

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:38.620
to old nicknames that are

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:40.397
a little embarrassing but not too

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:41.520
embarrassing.

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:43.860
And that's the spirit

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:46.660
of the marshelik who

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:49.309
is trying to lighten

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:51.700
the mood and also keep things real,

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:54.620
and not leave it only

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:57.900
at the religious precepts that

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:00.150
his job is also partly to

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.129
impart to the couple.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:05.369
So here we have a

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:07.300
marshelik who is addressing

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:10.089
only the male members of the wedding party.

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:12.609
This is before the groom

00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:14.540
is taken to meet

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:17.770
the bride and proceed with the ceremony.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:21.180
So this is all men, this is the

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:24.340
the groom and his father

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.769
principly, and

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:29.385
the other members of
the, male members of the

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.940
community and

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:34.460
the marshelik begins by

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:35.780
saying

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:41.220
[VOCALIZES]

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:42.580
to Shmelke.

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:44.239
That's the groom.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:47.489
His dad says

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:50.500
[VOCALIZES].

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:54.010
Don't try to be a wise
guy, don't try to be,

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:55.380
play the fool.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:57.459
Don't pretend that you
don't understand what's

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:58.460
going on.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:13:59.860
That's what his dad is saying.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:02.620
And then the marshelik continues,

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:06.020
[VOCALIZES].

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:09.379
Listen to what your old man is saying.

00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:12.900
Horkh oys dayn foter Yankl dem alten.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:17.740
[VOCALIZES].

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:20.740
And your anger at me

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:22.620
contain it.

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:24.353
Now just,

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:27.420
hold yourself back here.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:29.619
Don't be angry with me, I'm just the

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:30.620
messenger.

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:32.779
Very interesting.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:35.290
Why is the groom angry?

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:37.390
What's the anger about?

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:38.780
Class?

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:41.210
The anger is that

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:45.170
the groom is about to
lose his independence,

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:46.670
no longer going to be

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:49.430
a free, single

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:53.580
young person with no

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:55.369
responsibilities.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:58.249
And, no, he's going

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:00.439
to have to become a
full fledged member of the

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:03.259
community, take on all the obligations

00:15:03.530 --> 00:15:05.681
of marriage and

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:09.330
that's a scary transition,

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.229
as one he apparently

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:15.070
doesn't want to take on.

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:16.140
He's angry about it.

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:20.460
So the marshelik continues

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:23.099
to explain, "Er

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:25.860
ken nisht esn," [VOCALIZES].

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:28.180
He can't eat, [VOCALIZES],

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:29.639
he can't sleep.

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.579
He can't eat, he can't sleep, he's so

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:33.580
agitated.

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:36.479
This formulation is one that we find

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.820
in another

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.489
context of romantic songs,

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:43.169
a love song where,

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:46.700
for example, in "A Kholem,"

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:50.500
we hear, "Ikh es nisht, di trink nisht

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:53.300
ikh shlaf nisht durkh di nakht.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.820
Wayl banken luba benk ikh

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.260
nakh dir nakh dir."

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:01.169
I can't eat, I can't sleep, I'm not

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:03.239
eating, sleeping, I'm
not sleeping through the

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:04.240
night. I can't eat.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:07.259
I can't drink because I'm

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:09.690
longing for you, my love,

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:12.899
my sweetheart.

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:15.700
This is a different kind of not being

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.269
able to eat or sleep.

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:20.479
It's being upset and anxious,

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:22.910
the transition about to happen.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:25.850
And what's causing the anxiety?

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:31.220
[VOCALIZES]. Because it's written,

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:35.820
[VOCALIZES], it's written

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:38.500
in the Torah and the sacred law,

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:41.660
[VOCALIZES]

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:46.100
so says our creator,

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:50.620
[VOCALIZES],

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:54.020
a Jew must not

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:56.710
remain alone, must not be alone.

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:01.580
[VOCALIZES], a wife

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:04.420
he has to take, he has to have

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:05.500
a wife.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.500
And now comes the punchline:

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:12.260
[VOCALIZES].

00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:16.060
Who will be able, the wife, right, will

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:18.180
be able later to bury him.

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:23.060
[VOCALIZES]

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:26.340
A Jew must not be alone.

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:28.520
So that's the precept.

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:30.739
And this

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:34.450
this sort of double entendre, "zol

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:36.860
im kenen shpeter bagrubn,"

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:39.639
literally means, "will they be

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:41.170
able to bury him,"

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:45.460
which is a bit like...getting

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:48.660
married and having children

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:50.980
so that you will have a kaddishel,

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.660
you will have someone who can

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:55.620
say Kaddish after you.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:57.820
Uh.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:01.099
Yes, if you get married, then

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:04.380
when you die, your partner will be able

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:06.239
to make sure that you are brought to

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:08.159
keyver-yisro'el, that you were buried

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:09.660
according to Jewish law,

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:13.700
but also, also

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:16.519
it means to vanquish,

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.780
if you, if you bury someone, it's

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:21.401
like on the battlefield here.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:22.829
It's the battle of the sexes.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.689
Where you bury someone

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:27.569
who means that you vanquished, then you

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:28.570
conquer them.

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.780
So it's

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:33.900
a kind of wisdom

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:36.810
and wisecrack rolled into one.

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:40.860
A seykhl khokhme,

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:43.540
un a khokhme, a piece of

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:45.660
wisdom, but also a wisecrack.

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:49.599
And this is, this is the way

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:52.480
of the Marshelik, who was entertaining,

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:55.119
but also fulfilling

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.519
his obligation to lay down

00:18:57.520 --> 00:18:59.889
the law and make sure that

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:02.439
the participants of the wedding

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.180
understand what's, what's in store for

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:07.255
them and what they're getting into.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:11.829
We have in

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:14.500
the literature, for example, Itzik Manger

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:17.780
writes about the badkhen, who

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.279
is in that case addressing

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.710
the bride. And this is another convention

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:25.889
of explaining

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:28.229
how sad this day

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:30.620
is for the bride, because like

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:32.579
the groom who was about to lose his

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.189
independence, the bride is about to lose

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:36.460
her beauty.

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:38.699
So we have, for example,

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:41.579
in that song that I'm thinking

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:42.580
of from Manger,

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:48.260
"A shaten dos lebn,"

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:50.380
life is a shadow.

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:53.359
"A shaten dos glick," and

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:54.724
happiness is a shadow.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.980
"Di sheynkayt fergayt,"

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:01.580
beauty fades like the setting sun.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:04.580
"Um komt nisht tsurik,"

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:07.100
and doesn't return.

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:10.180
"Azoy zagt dos badkhen,"

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.340
that's what the badkhen, the marshelik

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:16.500
says, "mit trerin

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:19.060
im blick," with tears

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:20.380
in his eyes.

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:23.180
So in that case, Itzik Manger is

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:25.369
painting a little

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:28.780
portrait of a different
moment in the ritual

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:30.509
where the badkhen is

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:34.710
addressing the bride and

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:37.940
the marshelik song that, that

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:40.869
Jack M. remembers from growing

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:43.580
up. It's the marshelik

00:20:43.740 --> 00:20:46.589
having a little fun with reminding

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:49.620
the groom, "A

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:52.020
yidl tur nisht zayn alayn."

00:20:52.763 --> 00:20:54.773
You know, you've got
to get married because a

00:20:54.783 --> 00:20:56.233
Jew cannot be alone.

